commit_report_chapter_batch({'chapters': [{'chapter_id': 1, 'bundle_token': 
'b1-7d44c0c6', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor is the name used for a gold 
signet ring catalogued in the *Corpus der minoischen und mykenischen Siegel* as
CMS VI, no. 277. It entered scholarly literature through Arthur Evans’s 1925 
publication, which associated the object with a gold-ring and bead-seal 
treasure from Thisbê in Boeotia and interpreted its engraved scene in relation 
to a Minoan afterlife. [6] [10] [13]\n\nThe ring’s archaeological status is 
unsettled. One account reports that it was found in 1924 and that Evans 
accepted it as authentic, whereas many other scholars regarded it as a modern 
forgery; the same account records later renewed arguments for authenticity. [2]
The alleged find circumstances have also been judged suspicious, and another 
archaeological discussion describes the provenance as an alleged discovery in a
tholos tomb at Kakovatos near Pylos rather than as a securely documented 
excavation. [2] [9] Iconographic analysis has focused on comparisons with 
Egyptian prototypes and on the butterfly motif, which Marinatos and Jackson 
present as evidence against authenticity. [2] A separate 2013 study is 
represented in the supplied material as offering an authenticity-supporting 
interpretation, but its detailed argument is not available in the record used 
here. [3]', 'evidence_refs': ['e2', 'e3', 'e4', 'e5', 'e6', 'e36', 'e43', 
'e49', 'e59', 'e60'], 'disclosed_conflict_refs': ['x1', 'x2', 'x3']}, 
{'chapter_id': 2, 'bundle_token': 'b2-2a22f5d1', 'content': '## Discovery and 
provenance\n\nThe earliest published account links the object to a group of 
gold rings and gold bead-seals found in a Mycenaean rock tomb near Thisbê in 
Boeotia. The account dates the find to 1915 and describes it as having occurred
during wartime conditions, when discovery was diverted from official 
archaeological channels. [13] It states that a peasant found the material in a 
rock-cut chamber tomb near the village of Dombrena. W. A. Heurtley was shown 
three chamber tombs with dromoi in an olive grove, but the published extract 
withholds some details on grounds of confidentiality and does not establish 
that the treasure came from all three tombs. [13]\n\nThis account differs from 
a later description that calls the provenance an alleged discovery in a tholos 
tomb at Kakovatos near Pylos. The difference cannot be reconciled from the 
supplied evidence. The more cautious conclusion is that the ring has a 
reported, but not decisively documented, archaeological provenance; the 2019 
discussion explicitly places it among objects whose affiliation with Bronze Age
material is weakened by the absence of professional, well-documented 
excavations. [9] [13]\n\nThe ring first appeared in scholarly literature in 
1925. By that time Arthur Evans had privately purchased it and published it in 
the *Journal of Hellenic Studies*. He also issued a separate London edition, 
*The Ring of Nestor: A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and a Sepulchral 
Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia*, published 
by Macmillan in 1925; the National Library of Ireland catalog records that the 
book was issued separately, by permission, from an April 1925 paper in the 
journal. [9] [11] The supplied institutional catalog records concern copies of 
Evans’s book, not a documented chain of custody or current institutional holder
