commit_report_chapter_batch({'chapters': [{'chapter_id': 3, 'bundle_token': 
'b3-a80706c8', 'content': '## Description and iconography\n\nThe Ring of Nestor
is described as a gold signet ring and is identified in the supplied scholarly 
material as CMS VI, no. 277. A review of the Ashmolean seal corpus groups 
catalogue numbers 277–281 among gold rings with cult scenes. [9] [10] The 
supplied material does not provide a securely attributable physical measurement
for no. 277. Measurements of 2.1 cm by 1.2 cm are explicitly assigned to no. 
278, while an AI-generated summary gives 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm for the Nestor ring
without an accompanying original-text verification. [10] [21] The mass of 31.8 
g and the inventory number AN1938.1130 are associated with an Ashmolean object 
in secondary catalog data, but the same data give CMS VI.229 rather than CMS 
VI.277, so these details cannot safely be treated as a verified description of 
the ring discussed here. [22] [23]\n\nEvans interpreted the imagery on the 
bezel as a Minoan Elysian domain, an afterlife scene. [9] The later 
iconographic discussion summarized in the supplied material identifies a key 
figure previously called Evans’s “watch-dog” or “dragon” as a scorpion and 
treats it as an apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean iconography. [6] Another
study, by Nanno Marinatos and Briana Jackson, relates the image’s coherence to 
Egyptian prototypes and argues that the use of a butterfly motif weighs against
authenticity. [2] The supplied record also contains an opposing 2013 
iconographic argument associated with Theodore Eliopoulos, but the available 
text is a landing-page summary rather than the paper’s full analysis; its claim
that incurved altars support authenticity therefore requires caution. [3]\n\nA 
photograph caption identifies a Ring of Nestor displayed at the Ashmolean 
Museum in Oxford in 2025, with the Ashmolean named as the image source, but the
caption is not a full curatorial record. [7] The Ashmolean corpus review also 
records an editorial duplication in which a photograph of the Nestor ring 
appears on a page devoted to Evans’s replicas of the Ring of Minos. [10]', 
