──────────────────────────────── Final Report ─────────────────────────────────
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                               Ring of Nestor                                │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

The Ring of Nestor is the name used in archaeological literature for a gold    
signet ring associated with Aegean Bronze Age studies and identified in        
scholarly discussions as CMS VI, no. 277.[1][2] Its publication history begins 
with Sir Arthur Evans’s 1925 study, The Ring of Nestor: A Glimpse into the     
Minoan After-World and a Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and          
Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia; a related notice by V. Gordon Childe appeared 
in Nature in 1925.[3][4] Evans interpreted the scene on the bezel as           
representing a Minoan Elysian domain.[5]                                       

The reported provenance is not consistent across the surviving accounts.       
Evans’s account connected the associated treasure with a Mycenaean rock-cut    
tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia, with a find date of 1915, whereas another         
scholarly account describes the ring as allegedly found in a tholos tomb at    
Kakovatos near Pylos; a separate abstract gives 1924 as the find year.[6][7][8]
The authenticity of the object has consequently remained disputed: Evans       
accepted it as genuine, while other assessments have treated it as a modern    
forgery; later publications have again argued both for and against             
authenticity, including arguments based on Egyptian prototypes and the         
butterfly motif.[9][10]                                                        


                           Discovery and provenance                            


                           Discovery and provenance                            

The earliest published account links the object to a group of gold rings and   
gold bead-seals found in a Mycenaean rock tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia. The     
account dates the find to 1915 and describes it as having occurred during      
wartime conditions, when discovery was diverted from official archaeological   
channels. [13] It states that a peasant found the material in a rock-cut       
chamber tomb near the village of Dombrena. W. A. Heurtley was shown three      
chamber tombs with dromoi in an olive grove, but the published extract         
withholds some details on grounds of confidentiality and does not establish    
that the treasure came from all three tombs. [13]                              

This account differs from a later description that calls the provenance an     
alleged discovery in a tholos tomb at Kakovatos near Pylos. The difference     
cannot be reconciled from the supplied evidence. The more cautious conclusion  
is that the ring has a reported, but not decisively documented, archaeological 
provenance; the 2019 discussion explicitly places it among objects whose       
affiliation with Bronze Age material is weakened by the absence of             
professional, well-documented excavations. [9] [13]                            

The ring first appeared in scholarly literature in 1925. By that time Arthur   
Evans had privately purchased it and published it in the Journal of Hellenic   
Studies. He also issued a separate London edition, The Ring of Nestor: A       
Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and a Sepulchral Treasure of Gold          
Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia, published by Macmillan in    
1925; the National Library of Ireland catalog records that the book was issued 
separately, by permission, from an April 1925 paper in the journal. [9] [11]   
The supplied institutional catalog records concern copies of Evans’s book, not 
a documented chain of custody or current institutional holder for the physical 
ring. [11] [12]                                                                


                          Description and iconography                          


                          Description and iconography                          

The Ring of Nestor is described as a gold signet ring and is identified in the 
supplied scholarly material as CMS VI, no. 277. A review of the Ashmolean seal 
corpus groups catalogue numbers 277–281 among gold rings with cult scenes. [9] 
[10] The supplied material does not provide a securely attributable physical   
measurement for no. 277. Measurements of 2.1 cm by 1.2 cm are explicitly       
assigned to no. 278, while an AI-generated summary gives 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm for
the Nestor ring without an accompanying original-text verification. [10] [21]  
The mass of 31.8 g and the inventory number AN1938.1130 are associated with an 
Ashmolean object in secondary catalog data, but the same data give CMS VI.229  
rather than CMS VI.277, so these details cannot safely be treated as a verified
description of the ring discussed here. [22] [23]                              

Evans interpreted the imagery on the bezel as a Minoan Elysian domain, an      
afterlife scene. [9] The later iconographic discussion summarized in the       
supplied material identifies a key figure previously called Evans’s “watch-dog”
or “dragon” as a scorpion and treats it as an apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age  
Aegean iconography. [6] Another study, by Nanno Marinatos and Briana Jackson,  
relates the image’s coherence to Egyptian prototypes and argues that the use of
a butterfly motif weighs against authenticity. [2] The supplied record also    
contains an opposing 2013 iconographic argument associated with Theodore       
Eliopoulos, but the available text is a landing-page summary rather than the   
paper’s full analysis; its claim that incurved altars support authenticity     
therefore requires caution. [3]                                                

A photograph caption identifies a Ring of Nestor displayed at the Ashmolean    
Museum in Oxford in 2025, with the Ashmolean named as the image source, but the
caption is not a full curatorial record. [7] The Ashmolean corpus review also  
records an editorial duplication in which a photograph of the Nestor ring      
appears on a page devoted to Evans’s replicas of the Ring of Minos. [10]       


                              Authenticity debate                              


                              Authenticity debate                              

The authenticity question was present from the object’s entry into modern      
scholarship. The 1925 publication associated with Sir Arthur Evans records his 
acceptance of the ring as authentic, while later summaries report that other   
scholars condemned it as a forgery.[1][2] A discussion of the object’s early   
history states that it first appeared in scholarly literature in 1925 after    
Evans had privately purchased it and published it in the Journal of Hellenic   
Studies. The same account reports that members of the archaeological board in  
Athens had refused to buy it from its owner because they considered it the work
of a modern artist.[3] These reports document competing historical judgments,  
but they do not by themselves establish the object’s authenticity or falsity.  

Arguments against authenticity have focused on both provenance and iconography.
Nanno Marinatos and Briana Jackson, in their 2011 article, characterize the    
circumstances of the find as suspicious and argue that the composition depends 
on Egyptian prototypes; they further state that the use of the butterfly motif 
weighs against authenticity.[4][5] Their abstract does not reproduce the       
detailed comparative analysis or specify all of the circumstances on which the 
judgment rests. The absence of a professionally documented excavation context  
has also been treated as a reason for caution in assessing the ring’s          
attribution to Bronze Age material.[3]                                         

Arguments in favor of authenticity have been advanced through later            
iconographic reassessment. An abstract of A. G. Vlachopoulos’s 2020 study      
identifies the object as CMS VI, no. 277 and describes a reassessment of cases 
in which Cretan-Mycenaean gold rings had been erroneously classified as fakes; 
the abstract states that comparable wall paintings, gold rings, figurines and  
ivories contributed to that reassessment.[6] Vlachopoulos also proposes that   
Evans’s “watch-dog” or “dragon” should be identified as a scorpion, an         
apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean iconography.[7] These arguments         
represent a scholarly interpretation rather than a conclusive scientific       
authentication. The available record therefore supports describing the Ring of 
Nestor as an object whose ancient attribution and authenticity remain          
unresolved.                                                                    


